; Array and structure codegen test.
;
; DELIBERATELY IDENTICAL on all six classic 8-bit targets, the same way
; z80_byte / m6809_byte are, so a divergence between two of them is the backend
; and not the program.
;
; Every check here is a VALUE read back out of RAM after execution. A test that
; only compiles proves nothing about an address calculation: a wrong element
; scale or a wrong base still assembles perfectly and writes to the wrong
; place. That is the whole reason this file exists rather than a build check.
;
; What each block pins, and why it is here rather than something simpler:
;   aw[]    CONSTANT and VARIABLE index on the same array. The variable index
;           is what exercises the multiply-by-element-size and the add; a
;           constant index can be right while the variable path is broken.
;   loop    the index arriving from a live loop counter - the shape a real
;           program uses, and the one that first exposes a base address
;           clobbered between the GET_ADDRESS and the load.
;   ab[]    a ONE-byte element, the width whose address arithmetic does NOT
;           have to scale, so it fails independently of the .w case.
;   al[]    a FOUR-byte element: scale 4, and the element move is the 32-bit
;           one. A backend can pass every .w case with a broken scale.
;   p\      a structure field, which reaches the same store through the
;           compile-time "#Z@name+offset" address form rather than a computed
;           one. Two different address paths, one instruction.
;   guard   the variable declared straight after the arrays. If an array
;           overruns its storage - which it did on every one of these backends
;           until slots were sized from the symbol table - this is what dies,
;           and it dies silently unless something reads it.
EnableExplicit

Structure Pt
  x.w
  y.w
EndStructure

Global r_const.w
Global r_var.w
Global r_sum.w
Global r_last.w
Global r_byte.w
Global r_longlo.w
Global r_fx.w
Global r_fsum.w
Global r_neigh.w

Dim aw.w[5]
Dim ab.b[4]
Dim al.l[3]
Global p.Pt
Global guard.w

Procedure Main()
  Define i.w
  Define s.w

  guard = 1234

  aw[0] = 11
  aw[1] = 22
  aw[2] = 33
  aw[3] = 44
  aw[4] = -5

  r_const = aw[2] + aw[3]      ; 77   - both indices are constants
  i = 1
  r_var = aw[i] * 10           ; 220  - the index is a VARIABLE

  s = 0
  i = 0
  While i < 5
    s = s + aw[i]
    i = i + 1
  Wend
  r_sum = s                    ; 105  = 11+22+33+44-5

  i = 4
  r_last = aw[i]               ; -5   - a negative element via a variable index

  ab[0] = 100
  ab[1] = 100
  i = 1
  ab[2] = ab[0] + ab[i]        ; 200 in a signed byte, i.e. -56
  ab[3] = 0 - ab[2]            ; 56, negated at BYTE width
  r_byte = ab[3]               ; 56
                               ; DELIBERATELY NOT "r_byte = ab[2]". That reads
                               ; a NEGATIVE .b element into a .w, and the
                               ; shared frontend zero-extends it (200) rather
                               ; than sign-extending it (-56) - on every
                               ; target, the AVR included, because
                               ; WidensUnsigned() resolves a type for an
                               ; identifier and for #AST_ARRAY_ACCESS but not
                               ; for the #AST_FIELD_ACCESS node an element read
                               ; actually produces, so it falls to the untyped
                               ; "unsigned" default. That is a frontend defect;
                               ; pinning it here would pin the wrong layer.
                               ; Negating at byte width gives a POSITIVE
                               ; result, which both extensions agree on, and
                               ; still proves the element held 0xC8.

  al[0] = 100000
  i = 2
  al[i] = al[0] + 7
  r_longlo = al[2] - 100000    ; 7 - a four-byte element, scale 4, narrowed to
                               ; a .w only so this file can be checked by a
                               ; 16-bit harness

  p\x = 10
  p\y = 32
  r_fx = p\x                   ; 10
  r_fsum = p\x + p\y           ; 42

  r_neigh = guard              ; 1234 if nothing wrote past an array's end
EndProcedure
Main()
